"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-14","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-20","committee":"Planning Policy & Climate Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Moved","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-19","committee":"Strategy & Resources Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-02","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-10","committee":"Strategy & Resources Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-02","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-15","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-15","committee":"The Constitution of St Albans City and District Council","time":"","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"New; Extraordinary - To consider the adoption of the Local Plan","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Planning Policy & Climate Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Moved","webcast":true,"url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"St Albans faces \u00a30.9m budget overspend as homelessness demand outpaces funding","lead":"St Albans City and District Council is forecast to overspend its 2026-27 budget by \u00a30.9m, with temporary accommodation for homeless households accounting for \u00a30.6m of the shortfall. The council increased its homelessness budget by \u00a31m this year but demand has continued to accelerate, rising 27 per cent in seven months to 244 households by the end of July, forcing greater reliance on higher-cost hotel placements. A further \u00a30.4m of the overspend is attributed to delays in signing leases at Jubilee Square, which threaten planned commercial rental income. If the forecast materialises, the council's general reserves will fall to only \u00a30.4m above its minimum required level of \u00a33.4m\u2014a buffer of just 10 per cent\u2014leaving minimal headroom for unexpected costs. The council has already drawn down departmental savings to offset some pressures, but councillors face a choice between managing the deficit through further cuts or allowing reserves to erode further.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Appendix 1 MTFS","paper_url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50078506/50078506.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"St Albans faces \u00a30.9m overspend this year as homelessness costs and commercial delays mount","lead":"St Albans City Council is forecasting a \u00a3900,000 overspend in its 2026-27 budget, driven by spiralling homelessness costs and delays in rental income from its Jubilee Square development. Temporary accommodation numbers surged 27 per cent in seven months to 244 households by July, forcing greater reliance on costlier hotel placements. The council increased its homelessness budget by \u00a31m this year but demand has continued to outstrip supply. The shortfall is also fuelled by \u00a3400,000 in delays to commercial leases at Francis Bacon House that remain unsigned. If the forecast materialises, the council's general reserves \u2014 money set aside as a financial cushion \u2014 will fall to just \u00a33.7m, leaving only \u00a3400,000 above the minimum level considered prudent. The council already ranks in the lower half of comparable authorities on reserves strength, leaving minimal headroom for unexpected costs.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Appendix 1 MTFS","paper_url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50078506/50078506.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"St Albans homelessness demand surges 27% in seven months, squeezing council finances","lead":"St Albans City Council is facing a surge in homelessness that threatens its financial resilience. The number of households in temporary accommodation rose from 192 in December 2025 to 244 by July 2026, a 27 per cent increase in seven months, with emergency hotel placements rising from 27 to 58. Despite a \u00a31m budget increase for 2026/27, demand continues to exceed available housing, forcing costlier reliance on hotels. The council is now forecasting a \u00a30.9m overspend for the year, with homelessness accounting for \u00a30.6m of that shortfall. If the overspend materialises, general reserves will fall to only \u00a30.4m above the minimum required level of \u00a33.4m\u2014a financial buffer the council's own papers describe as creating \"a financial risk for the Council if the forecast outturn deteriorates further.\" The 2027/28 budget includes a further \u00a30.2m for temporary accommodation costs, with an additional \u00a30.3m downside risk flagged.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Appendix 1 MTFS","paper_url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50078506/50078506.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","paper_title":"Treasury Management Mid Year report 202627","paper_url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50078642/50078642.pdf","headline":"Maturity structure of borrowing rated amber in mid-year review","detail":"The council's mid-year monitoring shows all treasury indicators rated green except one: the maturity structure of borrowing, which is rated amber. This is the only indicator flagged as requiring attention from the Audit and Governance Committee.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Maturity structure determines refinancing risk and cash-flow timing; concentration of debt maturities constrains financial flexibility and can force borrowing at disadvantageous rates.","figures":"RAG rating: one amber (maturity structure of borrowing), all others green","quote":"The indicators are RAG rated green, with the exception of the maturity structure of borrowing, which is rated amber.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"City Neighbourhoods Committee","paper_title":"Budget Report for CNC CTTEE Sept 2026","paper_url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50078541/50078541.pdf","headline":"Awaab's Law implementation will create new statutory obligations on housing hazards with uncertain but likely material cost implications.","detail":"Part 2 of Awaab's Law comes into effect November 2026, requiring the council to investigate and remedy a wider range of housing hazards within prescribed timescales. Part 3 follows in October 2027. The council has not yet quantified the financial impact but notes that additional costs may arise for responsive repairs, compliance activity, property inspections and staffing resources.","category":"demand","matters":"Statutory obligations backed by regulation create open-ended cost exposure that the Housing Revenue Account may not be able to absorb, particularly if it falls into deficit during this period.","figures":"No figures quantified; full financial implications still being assessed","quote":"The implementation of Awaab's Law Part 2 from November 2026 will increase the obligations placed on the Council to investigate and remedy a wider range of housing hazards within prescribed timescales. This may result in additional costs associated with responsive repairs, compliance activity, property inspections and staffing resources.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"City Neighbourhoods Committee","paper_title":"Budget Report for CNC CTTEE Sept 2026","paper_url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50078541/50078541.pdf","headline":"Local Government Reorganisation constraints will restrict the council's ability to initiate projects or respond to pressures above specified thresholds.","detail":"A Section 24 Direction is expected to require Shadow Board approval for any council proposals exceeding thresholds estimated at \u00a31m capital expenditure and \u00a30.1m revenue expenditure. This will limit the council's ability to introduce new initiatives, reduce flexibility to respond to emerging financial pressures, and require material decisions to align with the new unitary authority's priorities.","category":"governance","matters":"Loss of autonomous decision-making during a period of financial stress reduces a council's ability to respond rapidly to budget shortfalls or unexpected service demands, and creates uncertainty about what interventions are permissible.","figures":"Estimated thresholds: \u00a31m capital expenditure, \u00a30.1m revenue expenditure (not yet formally confirmed)","quote":"The anticipated Structural Changes Order (SCO) and associated Section 24 Direction are expected to restrict the Council's ability to initiate major new projects or make significant financial commitments.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"City Neighbourhoods Committee","paper_title":"Budget Report for CNC CTTEE Sept 2026","paper_url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50078541/50078541.pdf","headline":"Commercial income from Jubilee Square is delayed and uncertain, contributing \u00a30.4m to the 2026/27 overspend.","detail":"The council has planned \u00a30.3m of additional commercial income from Jubilee Square (Francis Bacon House lettings) as part of the 2027/28 MTFS, but leases have not yet been signed. Delays in completing these lettings are contributing \u00a30.4m to the 2026/27 in-year overspend and create ongoing uncertainty about whether the income will materialise.","category":"income","matters":"Delayed or uncertain income streams reduce budget headroom and indicate execution risk in the council's commercial strategy. Reliance on income that is not yet contracted creates balance sheet vulnerability.","figures":"\u00a30.3m expected additional income in 2027/28; \u00a30.4m attributed to lease delays contributing to 2026/27 overspend","quote":"The 2027/28 plan includes additional commercial income of \u00a30.3m from Jubilee Square associated with the phased letting of Francis Bacon House over the course of the MTFS.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"City Neighbourhoods Committee","paper_title":"Budget Report for CNC CTTEE Sept 2026","paper_url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50078541/50078541.pdf","headline":"The 2027/28 budget is vulnerable to inflation and could swing from a small \u00a378k surplus to a \u00a3909k deficit across tested scenarios.","detail":"Three sensitivity scenarios have been modelled for 2027/28. The baseline MTFS assumes 3.0% inflation and projects a \u00a30.078m surplus. However, Scenario 1 (2.5% inflation) projects a \u00a30.207m surplus, Scenario 2 holds at \u00a30.078m, and Scenario 3 (3.5% inflation plus additional homelessness costs and commercial income variations) projects a \u00a30.909m deficit. The council explicitly notes uncertainty in inflation (CPI was 2.6% in June, expected to reach 3.2% in October/November) and every 1% change impacts the non-staff budget by \u00a3345k.","category":"income","matters":"A \u00a31.1m swing between best and worst case scenarios indicates the budget has minimal margin for error. Given reserves are already depleted by 2026/27, any downside scenario forces either unplanned cuts or new borrowing.","figures":"Scenario 1 (2.5% inflation): \u00a3207k surplus; Scenario 2 (3.0% inflation): \u00a378k surplus; Scenario 3 (3.5% inflation): \u00a3909k deficit. Inflation sensitivity: 1% change = \u00a3345k impact on non-staff budget","quote":"Given the level of uncertainty in several of the assumptions outlined above, including the outturn for 2026/27, we have developed 3 scenarios for the Budget Gap shown below, showing the impact of different inflation, commercial rental income and homelessness costs.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"St Albans","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-23","since_iso":"2026-08-24","distress":false,"headline":"Awaab's Law implementation will create new statutory obligations on housing hazards with uncertain but likely material cost implications.","lead":"Statutory obligations backed by regulation create open-ended cost exposure that the Housing Revenue Account may not be able to absorb, particularly if it falls into deficit during this period.","also":["40 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Strategy & Resources Committee on 2026-09-24","n_findings":41,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Budget Report for CNC CTTEE Sept 2026","top_paper_url":"https://stalbans.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50078541/50078541.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":140,"news_drafts_total":3}}